<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Guides', href: '/docs/guides' }, { label: 'Wiring Notifications' }]}>
  <h1>Wiring Notifications</h1>

  <p>
    OpenFlows reaches out when it needs a human — most importantly for <strong>escalations</strong>
    (tickets that need a decision). This guide covers connecting your notification channels.
  </p>

  <h2>Why notifications matter</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows runs autonomously, but it is not unsupervised. When a ticket escalates to a human,
    you want to know promptly. Notifications turn "check the panel later" into "you were told
    now," and they include direct links to the workspace and pull request so you can act
    immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported channels</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows can notify you through <strong>Slack</strong>, <strong>Discord</strong>, and
    <strong>WhatsApp</strong>. You configure whichever channels your team uses. They can be
    combined.
  </p>

  <h2>How each channel is wired</h2>
  <p>Each channel needs its connection details supplied at setup:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Slack</strong> — a webhook URL for your workspace or channel.</li>
    <li><strong>Discord</strong> — a webhook URL.</li>
    <li><strong>WhatsApp</strong> — the Twilio account credentials and phone numbers (your sending number and the destination).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Once set, the channel is live for that team.</p>

  <h2>How notifications behave</h2>
  <p>Notifications are engineered to be <strong>useful, not noisy</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Actionable.</strong> Each one names the ticket, the reason, the role involved, and includes links to jump in.</li>
    <li><strong>Rate-limited.</strong> OpenFlows sends at most one notification per channel per ticket within a time window, preventing alert floods.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe.</strong> Notifications are fire-and-forget: if a channel is down, it's logged and never breaks the orchestration loop. Your team's work is unaffected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Get the channel's connection details</strong> — a webhook URL, or Twilio credentials and phone numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Set the details</strong> for your team or setup in the appropriate configuration.</li>
    <li><strong>Trigger a test escalation</strong> (or wait for a real one) and confirm the message arrives with correct links.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What you should see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A correctly formatted notification on your channel.</li>
    <li>It includes the ticket ID, the reason for escalation, and direct links.</li>
    <li>It doesn't repeat every few minutes — the rate limit holds.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Wire at least <strong>one</strong> channel from the start so you never miss an escalation.</li>
    <li>If you use several channels, you'll get the same escalation policy on each (each is rate-limited independently).</li>
    <li>Remember that notifications are only one surface — <strong>escalations always show in the control panel too</strong>, so nothing is lost if a channel is briefly unavailable.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info" title="What to do when a notification arrives">
    See <a href="/docs/guides/handling-human-escalations">Handling Human Escalations</a> for how
    to recognize and resolve a ticket that needs you.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
